# E3_lens_validation shared stimuli (exec'd inside daemon jobs and locally).
# PROSE: 24 wikitext-like encyclopedic paragraphs (~90-130 tokens). Tags: 'neutral' (no gendered person),
# 'bio_f' (female subject), 'bio_m' (male subject). Not copied from wikitext, but closely paraphrased/adapted
# from the lead sections of the corresponding English Wikipedia articles (https://en.wikipedia.org); the PROSE text
# is therefore shared under CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/). See NOTICE.
PROSE = [
 ("neutral", "The Danube is the second-longest river in Europe, after the Volga. It rises in the Black Forest of Germany and flows southeast for some 2,850 kilometres, passing through or along the borders of ten countries before draining into the Black Sea. Historically the river formed a long-standing frontier of the Roman Empire, and today it remains an important waterway for commercial shipping, connecting the industrial regions of central Europe with ports on the Black Sea."),
 ("neutral", "Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants, algae and some bacteria convert light energy into chemical energy. In the light-dependent reactions, which take place in the thylakoid membranes of the chloroplast, water is split and oxygen is released as a by-product. The energy captured is then used in the Calvin cycle to fix carbon dioxide into three-carbon sugars, which the organism uses to build glucose, starch and other organic compounds."),
 ("neutral", "The 1966 FIFA World Cup was the eighth edition of the tournament and was held in England from 11 to 30 July. Sixteen teams took part, and matches were played at eight stadiums across the country. The final, staged at Wembley Stadium in London, was decided in extra time, and the host nation won the trophy for the first time. Attendance across the tournament exceeded 1.6 million spectators, a record at that time."),
 ("neutral", "Basalt is a fine-grained volcanic rock formed from the rapid cooling of lava rich in magnesium and iron. It makes up most of the oceanic crust and is the most common rock type on the surface of the terrestrial planets. When thick basalt flows cool slowly, they can contract and crack into regular hexagonal columns, as seen at the Giant's Causeway in Northern Ireland and at Fingal's Cave on the Scottish island of Staffa."),
 ("neutral", "The printing press with movable type was developed in Mainz around 1440. By combining an adapted screw press with oil-based ink and metal type cast in reusable moulds, printers could produce books far more quickly and cheaply than scribes copying by hand. Within fifty years presses were operating in more than two hundred European cities, and the rapid spread of printed material is often credited with accelerating the Reformation and the Scientific Revolution."),
 ("neutral", "Monsoon climates are characterised by a seasonal reversal of the prevailing winds, which brings a pronounced wet season followed by a long dry season. The South Asian monsoon is the best known example: during summer, the land heats faster than the Indian Ocean, drawing moist air inland and producing heavy rainfall across India, Bangladesh and Nepal. Agriculture in the region depends heavily on the timing and strength of these rains."),
 ("neutral", "The Eiffel Tower is a wrought-iron lattice tower on the Champ de Mars in Paris. It was constructed between 1887 and 1889 as the entrance arch for the 1889 World's Fair. Although initially criticised by some of the city's leading artists and intellectuals, the tower has become a global cultural icon of France. It is 330 metres tall and was the tallest man-made structure in the world until the completion of the Chrysler Building in 1930."),
 ("neutral", "A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify or switch electrical signals. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The first working point-contact transistor was demonstrated at Bell Laboratories in 1947, and transistors soon replaced vacuum tubes in radios, calculators and computers. Modern integrated circuits may contain many billions of transistors on a single chip."),
 ("neutral", "The Great Barrier Reef is the world's largest coral reef system, stretching for over 2,300 kilometres off the coast of Queensland, Australia. It is composed of nearly three thousand individual reefs and hundreds of islands, and it can be seen from outer space. The reef supports a remarkable diversity of life, including many species of fish, molluscs and sea turtles, but rising sea temperatures have caused repeated mass bleaching events in recent decades."),
 ("neutral", "Sourdough bread is made by the fermentation of dough using naturally occurring lactobacilli and wild yeast. The lactic acid produced by the bacteria gives the bread its characteristic sour taste and improves its keeping qualities. A portion of the fermented dough, known as the starter, is retained after each baking and used to leaven the next batch. Sourdough was the usual form of leavening in Europe until the introduction of commercial baker's yeast."),
 ("neutral", "The Silk Road was a network of trade routes that connected East Asia with the Mediterranean world for more than fifteen centuries. Merchants carried silk, spices, paper and porcelain westward, while horses, glassware, wool and precious metals travelled east. Beyond goods, the routes enabled the exchange of ideas, religions and technologies, including the spread of Buddhism into China and the transmission of papermaking to the Islamic world and eventually to Europe."),
 ("neutral", "A tropical cyclone is a rapidly rotating storm system characterised by a low-pressure centre, strong winds and a spiral arrangement of thunderstorms. Depending on its location and strength, it may be called a hurricane, a typhoon or simply a cyclone. These storms draw their energy from warm ocean water, which is why they weaken quickly after making landfall. Storm surge, rather than wind, is usually responsible for the greatest loss of life."),
 ("neutral", "The violin is a wooden string instrument in the violin family, and the smallest and highest-pitched instrument in regular use in the family. It typically has four strings tuned in perfect fifths and is most commonly played by drawing a bow across the strings. The modern form of the instrument emerged in northern Italy in the early sixteenth century, and the workshops of Cremona produced some of the most highly valued examples ever made."),
 ("neutral", "Antarctica is the southernmost continent and contains the geographic South Pole. It is the fifth-largest continent, and about 98 percent of its surface is covered by ice that averages nearly two kilometres in thickness. Antarctica is, on average, the coldest, driest and windiest continent. There is no permanent human population, but several thousand people live at research stations operated by many countries under the terms of the Antarctic Treaty."),
 ("neutral", "The railway line between Liverpool and Manchester opened in 1830 and was the first inter-city railway in the world to rely exclusively on steam locomotives. It was also the first to be fully double-tracked, to have a signalling system, and to carry both passengers and freight according to a timetable. The success of the line encouraged a wave of railway construction across Britain during the following two decades."),
 ("neutral", "Vaccination works by exposing the immune system to a harmless form or component of a pathogen, prompting it to produce antibodies and memory cells. If the vaccinated person later encounters the real pathogen, the immune system can respond far more quickly. Large vaccination campaigns led to the global eradication of smallpox, declared in 1980, and have greatly reduced the incidence of diseases such as polio, measles and diphtheria."),
 ("neutral", "The Andes are the longest continental mountain range in the world, forming a continuous highland along the western edge of South America. The range is about 7,000 kilometres long and passes through seven countries. Its highest peak, Aconcagua, rises to nearly 7,000 metres above sea level. The Andes contain extensive mineral deposits, and the high plateaus have supported agriculture and large settlements since long before the arrival of Europeans."),
 ("neutral", "A lighthouse is a tower or other structure designed to emit light from a system of lamps and lenses, serving as a navigational aid for ships at sea. Lighthouses mark dangerous coastlines, hazardous shoals and reefs, and safe entries to harbours. The invention of the Fresnel lens in the early nineteenth century greatly increased the range of their beams. Today most lighthouses are automated, and many older towers have been converted into museums."),
 ("bio_f", "Marie Curie was a physicist and chemist who conducted pioneering research on radioactivity. She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize and remains the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different scientific fields. Born in Warsaw, she moved to Paris to continue her studies, where she later discovered the elements polonium and radium together with her husband. Her work laid the foundation for the development of X-ray machines in surgery."),
 ("bio_f", "Ada Lovelace was an English mathematician and writer, chiefly known for her work on Charles Babbage's proposed mechanical general-purpose computer, the Analytical Engine. She was the first to recognise that the machine had applications beyond pure calculation, and she published what is often regarded as the first algorithm intended to be carried out by such a machine. Her notes on the engine were largely forgotten until they were rediscovered in the twentieth century."),
 ("bio_f", "Wangari Maathai was a Kenyan environmental and political activist. In 1977 she founded the Green Belt Movement, an organisation that focused on planting trees, environmental conservation and women's rights. Over the following decades the movement planted tens of millions of trees across Kenya. In 2004 she became the first African woman to receive the Nobel Peace Prize, which was awarded for her contribution to sustainable development, democracy and peace."),
 ("bio_m", "Isaac Newton was an English mathematician, physicist and astronomer who is widely recognised as one of the most influential scientists of all time. His book Principia Mathematica, first published in 1687, laid the foundations of classical mechanics and formulated the laws of motion and universal gravitation. He also made seminal contributions to optics and shares credit with Gottfried Leibniz for developing the infinitesimal calculus."),
 ("bio_m", "Nelson Mandela was a South African anti-apartheid activist and politician who served as the first president of South Africa from 1994 to 1999. He was the country's first black head of state and the first elected in a fully representative democratic election. Before his presidency he spent twenty-seven years in prison, much of it on Robben Island. After his release in 1990 he led negotiations that brought an end to apartheid."),
 ("bio_m", "Ludwig van Beethoven was a German composer and pianist whose works remain among the most performed in the classical repertoire. His career is conventionally divided into early, middle and late periods. He began to lose his hearing in his late twenties, and by the last decade of his life he was almost completely deaf, yet he continued to compose, producing some of his most admired works, including the Ninth Symphony and the late string quartets."),
]

